and relevant target refs. Check remote state only where it matters.
- Preserve unrelated work. Stage only reviewed 
paths belonging to the requested change.
- Do not amend, rebase, reset, clean, squash, cherry-pick, force-push, 
move tags or delete refs without authorization. Before approved destructive history changes, record original refs and 
create the approved recovery artifact. Prefer additive corrections for published history.

## 
Commits

Keep commits coherent and independently understandable. Do not mix unrelated fixes, generated churn or 
secret-bearing files. Run relevant checks before committing and record useful validation in the commit, PR or evidence 
record.

Unless the repository specifies otherwise:

- Use a capitalized imperative subject, about 50 
characters, at most 72, with no final period.
- Separate an optional body with a blank line; wrap prose at 72 
columns.
- Explain why and the resulting behavior, material risks or compatibility effects. Do not narrate the 
diff.
- Omit the body when the subject fully explains a simple change.
- Add references, trailers, sign-offs, 
co-authors and disclosures only when accurate and required.

```text
Preserve the selected export 
format

Keep the user's format when reopening an existing export. Validate
the saved setting before starting 
the writer.

Refs: #123
```

## Branches

For an authorized branch operation, follow the project 
convention. Otherwise prefer `<type>/<short-slug>`, such as `fix/parser-timeout`. Keep private targets, secrets, 
undisclosed vulnerability details and sensitive customer information out of branch names.

## Pull requests and 
merge requests

Follow the repository template. Otherwise lead with the concrete problem and resulting behavior; 
add relevant validation and material risks. A simple change usually needs two short paragraphs. Use more structure 
when the change requires it:

1. Problem and resulting behavior.
2. Change and rationale.
3. Preserved 
invariants or compatibility constraints.
4. Validation performed and relevant checks not run, with reasons.
5. 
Risks, rollback or migration details.
6. Review guidance and accurate links/metadata.

Describe the final 
implementation for a reviewer who has not seen the conversation. Keep the title and description current when scope 
changes. Before requesting review, read the full diff, confirm base/head and commit list, required checks and 
approvals, and what feedback is needed. Do not hide a material change in a later comment.

For 
security-sensitive changes, establish disclosure status and the permitted route before adding details to Git or CI 
artifacts.

## Issues and review comments

Use the required template. State component/problem, 
environment, expected and observed behavior, supporting evidence and requested outcome.

Give one actionable 
finding per review comment: priority where supported, exact location, evidence, violated requirement, consequence and 
correction. Suggest a focused check where useful. Mark assumptions; do not combine approval with an unresolved 
blocker.

Resolve a discussion only when its correction or decision is verified and the workflow permits 
it.

## Security and publication

Do not include credentials, tokens, private URLs, unrelated personal 
data or undisclosed exploit details in public Git text or CI output. Follow the repository's current 
security/disclosure policy; use its private route for initial undisclosed reports.

Private reports, advisories, 
forks, public patches and publication have distinct scopes and timing. Preserve authorized disclosure decisions, 
affected versions and evidence. A local ready state is not permission to submit or publish.

## 
Releases

Version changes, release branches, tags, pushed tags, hosted releases and deployments are distinct 
operations. For each authorized operation, verify relevant refs, version/changelog, state, checks, signing, rollback 
and disclosure requirements. Approval for one does not imply the rest.

Report completed actions only with 
current receipts. Do not claim a remote review, release or deployment from local preparation alone.

## 
References

Consult current repository rules first. Useful background: [Git's commit 
guidance](https://git-scm.com/docs/user-manual#creating-good-commit-messages), [GitHub PR review guidance](https://docs
.github.com/en/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/proposing-changes-to-your-work-with-pull-requests/helping
-others-review-your-changes), and [GitLab merge 
requests](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/project/merge_requests/).
