## Implementation — Ponytail

Apply **YAGNI-style discipline**: omit 
machinery for hypothetical future use. Keep single-use helpers when clarity, testing, or an existing contract 
justifies them.

Prefer suitable existing code, then standard-library/native capabilities, installed 
dependencies, and finally minimal new code. Add a dependency only when its concrete benefit justifies its cost; do not 
force an unsuitable solution to save lines.

### Full — simplify while staying readable

Remove redundant 
variables, wrappers, branches, duplicated work, and scaffolding. Combine clearly related steps. Keep meaningful names, 
useful types, straightforward control flow, and helpful function boundaries.

### Ultra — remove more 
unnecessary structure

Collapse intermediate steps, repetitive structure, and helpers that add no current value. 
Use concise local names only when immediately clear; preserve descriptive public names. Do not rename, reformat, or 
code-golf solely to look shorter. If little is redundant, little should change.

### Worked example — 
implementation

Keep `User`, `fetchUser()`, `displayUser()`, error text, the filtered return object, and three 
console calls. Full and Ultra are independent rewrites of the baseline. These are field-type checks, not email-address 
validation or stricter API-ID semantics.

#### Before — standard implementation

```ts
type User = 
{
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};

async function fetchUser(userId: number): 
Promise<User> {
  if (!Number.isInteger(userId) || userId <= 0) {
    throw new Error("Invalid user ID");
  
}

  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new 
Error(
      `Failed to fetch user ${userId}: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`
    );
  }

  
const data = await response.json();

  if (
    typeof data !== "object" ||
    data === null ||
    
typeof data.id !== "number" ||
    typeof data.name !== "string" ||
    typeof data.email !== "string"
 
 ) {
    throw new Error("Invalid user response");
  }

  return {
    id: data.id,
    name: 
data.name,
    email: data.email,
  };
}

async function displayUser(userId: number): Promise<void> 
{
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(userId);

    console.log(`User ID: ${user.id}`);
    
console.log(`Name: ${user.name}`);
    console.log(`Email: ${user.email}`);
  } catch (error) {
    const 
message =
      error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Unknown error";

    console.error(`Unable to 
display user: ${message}`);
  }
}

displayUser(123);
```

